<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tagus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tagus is the longest river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, flowing westwards through central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and reaching the Atlantic at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it formed an approximate frontier zone between Christian kingdoms and Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> use it to mark the northern limit of an empire that at its height stretched from the Atlantic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> to the Tagus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tagus.jpg" alt="Tagus" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mheberger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tagus_Watershed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Tagus, called the Tajo in Spanish and the Tejo in Portuguese, is 1,007 km long. It rises at an elevation of 1,593 metres in the municipality of Frías de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a>, in the Sierra de Albarracín, part of the Montes Universales. It flows 816 km through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, then forms a border of 47 km between Spain and Portugal before running 145 km through Portugal. Aranjuez, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and Talavera de la Reina are its main Spanish cities, and Abrantes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem">Santarém</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> its main Portuguese ones. Its basin covers 80,600 square kilometres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name was Tagus, and its most probable origin is an Indo-European root meaning to drip. Its history is bound up with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, two cities of great historical weight in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Toledo was built on a hill about 100 m high around the meander known as the Torno del Tajo, which protected it from attack. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> the mouth of the Tagus carried intense commercial activity between northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> it was the main communications hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>, which extended through America, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The lower Tagus is seismically active, with major earthquakes recorded in 1309, 1531, 1755 and 1909.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The basin is the most populated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, with more than ten million inhabitants, and includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> metropolitan area and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> region. The river's estuary is one of the few inverted deltas in the world: about 15 km wide and 25 km long, it exits to the sea through an opening only 2 km wide and forms a large lagoon with shallow sand banks. In the second half of the 20th century engineering works regulated the flow for drinking water, irrigation, electricity and the cooling of nuclear power plants. Protected areas include the Alto Tajo Nature Park, Monfragüe National Park and the Tagus Estuary Natural Reserve.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tagus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The victory earned him the honorific al-Mansur, &quot;the Victorious&quot;, and in the following campaigns his forces devastated Extremadura, the <b>Tagus</b> valley and La Mancha, raiding to the walls of Toledo in the deepest Muslim penetration of Christian territory for generations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Celtiberians were a group of Celtic-speaking peoples of the eastern Meseta, the high plateau of central Spain, in the upper basins of the Ebro's southern tributaries, the Douro and the <b>Tagus</b>, roughly the modern provinces of Soria, Guadalajara, Zaragoza, Teruel and Cuenca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The thinly populated lands between the Duero and the <b>Tagus</b> were settled by free peasants and townsmen attracted by fueros, local charters such as those of Sepúlveda (1076) and Cuenca (around 1190), which granted personal freedom, land and self-government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its expansion to the <b>Tagus</b> and later the Guadiana laid foundations for the Crown of Castile, and the County of Portugal, granted by Alfonso VI to his son-in-law Henry of Burgundy, became the independent Kingdom of Portugal under Afonso Henriques, recognised by León in the Treaty of Zamora…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The valleys of the <b>Tagus</b>, Guadiana and Douro produced grain, wine and olive oil, the uplands supported sheep, cattle and the Lusitanian horses that Roman writers praised for their speed, and the coast supported fishing and the salting of fish.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…and above all after Vouillé, Goths settled more freely under their clan rulers, the reiks, who received dukedoms or comital offices, around Mérida, Barcelona, Seville, Toledo and Narbonne, the bases of Gothic power, and in the farming country between the upper Douro, Ebro and <b>Tagus</b>, the Campi Gothorum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
